You need to use the spread operator to preserve other fields:

```jsx
// ❌ Wrong - overwrites entire object
setUser({ name: 'John' });

// ✅ Correct - merges with existing state
setUser(prev => ({
  ...prev,
  name: 'John'
}));

// Or with multiple fields
setUser(prev => ({
  ...prev,
  name: 'John',
  age: 30
}));
```

Always use the functional update form when the new state depends on the previous state!

Here are the common array operations:

```jsx
const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

// Add item to end
setItems(prev => [...prev, newItem]);

// Add item to beginning
setItems(prev => [newItem, ...prev]);

// Remove item by id
setItems(prev => prev.filter(item => item.id !== idToRemove));

// Update item
setItems(prev => prev.map(item => 
  item.id === targetId 
    ? { ...item, field: newValue }
    : item
));

// Replace entire array
setItems(newArray);
```

Never mutate state directly with `.push()`, `.splice()`, etc!

React uses shallow comparison to detect changes. If you mutate state directly:

```jsx
// ❌ This won't trigger re-render!
const [items, setItems] = useState([1, 2, 3]);
items.push(4);
setItems(items); // Same reference!
```

React sees the same array reference and thinks nothing changed, so it doesn't re-render.

```jsx
// ✅ This creates new reference
setItems([...items, 4]); // New array!
```

Always create a new array/object when updating state.

Use `useReducer` when:

1. **Complex state logic** with multiple sub-values
2. **Next state depends on previous state**
3. **Multiple related updates**

```jsx
const reducer = (state, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT':
      return { count: state.count + 1 };
    case 'DECREMENT':
      return { count: state.count - 1 };
    case 'RESET':
      return { count: 0 };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { count: 0 });

// Usage
dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' });
```

`useState` is simpler for most cases. `useReducer` shines when you have complex state transitions.
